    {
      "questionText": "What is the result of <code>type(10 / 2)</code> in Python 3?",
      "options": [
        { "text": "<code>&lt;class 'int'&gt;</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "<code>&lt;class 'float'&gt;</code>", "isCorrect": true },
        { "text": "<code>&lt;class 'str'&gt;</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "<code>&lt;class 'bool'&gt;</code>", "isCorrect": false }
      ],
      "explanation": "The single slash <code>/</code> operator performs true division in Python 3, always returning a floating-point number (e.g., <code>5.0</code>)."
    },
    {
      "questionText": "How do you assign the same value <code>5</code> to three variables <code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, and <code>c</code> in one line?",
      "options": [
        { "text": "<code>a, b, c = 5</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "<code>a = 5, b = 5, c = 5</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "<code>a = b = c = 5</code>", "isCorrect": true },
        { "text": "<code>a & b & c = 5</code>", "isCorrect": false }
      ],
      "explanation": "Python allows chained assignment. The value <code>5</code> is assigned to <code>c</code>, which is assigned to <code>b</code>, which is assigned to <code>a</code>."
    },
    {
      "questionText": "Which of the following statements regarding Python variables is TRUE?",
