        { "text": "Variable names can start with a special character like <code>@</code>.", "isCorrect": false }
      ],
      "explanation": "Python allows you to change the type of data a variable holds simply by assigning it a new value of a different type."
    },
    {
      "questionText": "What is the output of <code>print(\"Python\" * 3)</code>?",
      "options": [
        { "text": "<code>PythonPythonPython</code>", "isCorrect": true },
        { "text": "<code>Python3</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "<code>Error</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "<code>Python Python Python</code>", "isCorrect": false }
      ],
      "explanation": "The multiplication operator <code>*</code>, when used with a string and an integer, repeats the string that many times without adding spaces."
    },
    {
      "questionText": "What is the result of <code>int(\"10\") + 5</code>?",
      "options": [
        { "text": "<code>\"105\"</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "<code>15</code>", "isCorrect": true },
        { "text": "<code>105</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "Error", "isCorrect": false }
      ],
      "explanation": "<code>int(\"10\")</code> converts the string <code>\"10\"</code> into the integer <code>10</code>. Then, <code>10 + 5</code> is performed mathematically, resulting in <code>15</code>."
    },
    {
      "questionText": "Which of the following is considered a Float?",
      "options": [
        { "text": "<code>10</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "<code>2e3</code>", "isCorrect": true },
        { "text": "<code>\"4.5\"</code>", "isCorrect": false },
        { "text": "<code>False</code>", "isCorrect": false }
      ],
      "explanation": "Scientific notation (using <code>e</code> or <code>E</code> for power of 10) creates a floating-point number in Python. <code>2e3</code> is equivalent to <code>2000.0</code>."
